## PHASE 4 — MODERNIZE THE COMPLETE LEARNING JOURNEY

Ensure the documentation thoroughly explains the following topics in a logical order.

### A. Introduction and architecture

Explain the overall purpose of Rental-Ops-Manager, its users, its major features, and how the frontend, PHP backend, and MySQL/MariaDB database interact.

Explain API requests, responses, authentication, and persistence.

### B. Authentication and owner management

Explain the current login process, session tokens, authorization checks, account restrictions, login failures, owner management, and logout behavior.

Verify the currently supported roles.

### C. Booking and pricing

Explain customer selection, services, packages, optional add-ons, discounts, fees, negotiated totals, booking snapshots, and saving/updating bookings.

Document the actual lifecycle states and valid transitions.

### D. Availability management

Explain event time intervals, preparation and travel allowances, overlapping bookings, conflict resolution, approved overrides, availability blocks, and reconciliation.

Distinguish implemented code from deferred or unauthorized functionality.

### E. Payments and deposits

Explain rental payments, payment statuses, partial payments, corrections, outstanding balances, refundable deposits, itemized deductions, refund records, and the differences between system records and actual money transfers.

### F. Equipment and checklists

Explain package-based templates, checklist snapshots, outgoing inspections, release quantities, returned quantities, equipment condition, damaged/missing items, and post-event reviews.

### G. Post-event completion

Provide a clear end-to-end explanation of the actual lifecycle:

Confirmed booking → Awaiting Completion → Post-event review → Awaiting Payment or Completed.

Explain when the rental service becomes available again, how outstanding balances affect completion, and how notifications are resolved.

Verify all rules against source code.

### H. Offline synchronization

Explain how supported operations are stored locally, replayed to the backend, checked for conflicts, acknowledged, retried, and synchronized across devices.

Cover operation receipts, duplicate prevention, local/server IDs, stale-edit protection, and offline limitations.

### I. Reports and audit history

Explain how dashboard and report metrics are calculated, including date-filter behavior, completed payments, outstanding balances, cancellation metrics, deposits, approved overlaps, and audit records.

Distinguish operational reporting from formal accounting.

### J. Frontend

Explain the purpose of major admin screens, the JavaScript API layer, validation, user interactions, and how frontend operations connect to backend services.

### K. Database

Document every table actually defined in the current schema, including its columns, relationships, constraints, and role in the application.

Explain the database progressively before introducing detailed field dictionaries.

### L. Deployment and limitations

Explain the system's runtime requirements, the distinction between development and production, and relevant deployment limitations.

Do not claim production readiness merely because local tests passed.

